FALSIFIED-AND-DROPPED:
  - A compromised Tor publisher can supply a higher- or same-version dependency while the correct Tor hash still passes — install-tor.sh:29-34 excludes Tor's source for both dependency download and installation. tests/contract/security-decisions/tor_package_integrity_test.sh:83-102 and 171-176 challenge both version cases through real offline APT; the preserved pre-fix red log identifies the actual bypass. EVALUATED-AS: bash tests/contract/security-decisions/tor_package_integrity_test.sh must pass both dependency controls; removing either source restriction must fail before its installation marker.
  - The final APT operation can refetch unchecked Tor bytes despite a local-file argument — install-tor.sh:34 applies --no-download and uses the same private cache; the dependency phase has only the immutable Debian snapshot source. image-build-r2.log:319-370 shows one Tor download/check, snapshot-only dependency downloads and subsequent Tor unpacking, with no second Tor fetch. EVALUATED-AS: the fixture's exact local-file/offline assertions and a real wrong-hash build must continue to reject bypasses.
  - A non-executable new test is absent from the green gate — scripts/verify.sh:223-226 discovers by filename and invokes with bash; verify-r2.log explicitly records tor_package_integrity_test.sh PASS.
  - Old live successes are silently promoted into current full-image/integration proof — evidence-r2.json explicitly identifies their round-1 origin and sampled-content comparison limits; T161:54-75 keeps required live behavior and deployment/release promotion blocked. T155/T078/T079 directly depend on T161.
  - The threat model still calls independent Tor hash enforcement missing — THREAT-MODEL.md:1407-1421 replaces that stale residue with the implemented check, its review/delivery qualification, upstream-trust limitation and pending T161 obligation.

